data.content:
These are two distinct C# 15 breaking changes:

---

**1. `DynamicOps.cs` — `&&` with `dynamic` now partially binds at compile time**

In C# 15, when one operand of `&&`/`||` is `dynamic`, the compiler now resolves `operator true`/`false` at **compile time** on the non-dynamic operand's static type. Previously, the entire operation was deferred to runtime.

`IFilter` declares `operator true`/`false` but **no `operator &`**, so `&&` can't be constructed. Fixes:

```csharp
// Option A: cast both to dynamic (fully runtime-bound)
var combined = (dynamic)left && right;

// Option B: add operator & to the interface
interface IFilter
{
    static bool operator true(IFilter x) => true;
    static bool operator false(IFilter x) => false;
    static IFilter operator &(IFilter x, IFilter y) => x;
}
```

---

**2. `RefHelper.cs` — CS0518 with `ref readonly` returning lambda**

C# 15 changed how `ref readonly` returning lambdas are emitted, now requiring the well-known type `System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RequiresLocationAttribute` to be available. If your target framework or runtime references don't include it, you get CS0518.

Fixes:

```csharp
// Option A: use a named method instead of a lambda
static ref readonly int GetValue() => ref value;

// Option B: if stuck on an older TFM, define the attribute yourself
namespace System.Runtime.CompilerServices
{
    [AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Parameter)]
    internal sealed class RequiresLocationAttribute : Attribute { }
}
```

The preferred fix for #2 is to ensure you're targeting `net11.0`, which includes the attribute in the BCL.
